# Auth and RBAC

## JWT

- Login is public (`[AllowAnonymous]`). Other APIs require authentication + permission policies.
- Token claims: `NameIdentifier`, `Name`, `Role`, repeated `"permission"` claims from RoleRights.
- Permissions are a **snapshot until token expiry** (~30 min). Service-layer rules still protect system resources.
- Pass `CancellationToken` through login, user, role, and right service methods.

## Permissions

- Seeded rights: `User_*`, `Role_View`, `Role_Create`, `Role_Edit`, `Role_Delete`, `RoleRight_Assign`, `Right_View`.
- Register policies from `Permissions.All` in `Program.cs`.
- Controllers use `[Authorize(Policy = Permissions.X)]`. Handler requires authenticated user, a role claim, and matching permission claim.
- Role-right changes compare submitted right names with `CallerContext.PermissionNames` (the token snapshot).
- Do **not** hardcode business role names (`Admin`, `User`, `Coordinator`, …) in authorization logic.

## SuperAdmin / system protection

- Exactly one seeded system user and one system role: `User.IsSystem` / `Role.IsSystem`.
- Never trust `IsSystem` from request DTOs; always load from DB. Role responses omit the flag.
- Forbid: delete system user; change system user’s role; assign system role to anyone; edit, delete, or change rights of the system role.
- Reads of the system role, including its rights, return 404. Writes return 403.
- `PUT /api/users/{id}` rejects any caller, including the system user, updating their own account. Self-service profile changes are not this endpoint.
- User create and update reject a role whose rights are not a subset of `CallerContext.PermissionNames`. Update also rejects a target user whose current role is outside that ceiling.
- `RoleAssignment` holds the small user-write helpers, including the permission-ceiling check. No Admin/User hierarchy. System-role checks live in `RoleService`.
